/**
 * Ground truth for the retrieval evaluation (PROJECT.md 8.8).
 *
 * The corpus is scripted rather than generated by an LLM so that every answer
 * is known exactly: each question points at the planted messages that answer it,
 * and nothing else counts. That makes the scores a conservative lower bound -
 * a retriever that surfaces a genuinely useful filler message gets no credit.
 *
 * Questions are written the way a teammate would type them into `@bot`, not
 * reverse-engineered from the answers. Their categories exist to test the
 * prediction 8.8 asks to confirm or refute:
 *
 *   identifier  the answer hinges on a rare literal token present in both the
 *               question and the answer - a ticket, host, error code, version or
 *               company name. Predicted to favour keyword search.
 *   paraphrase  question and answer share no content words. Predicted to favour
 *               vector search.
 *   lexical     they share ordinary words, with no rare identifier.
 *
 * Every planted fact also has hand-written near misses in DISTRACTORS: messages
 * on the same topic that do not answer. Without them, vector search would look
 * far better than it is - a probe showed "we should deploy the release on monday
 * morning" scoring 0.66 against "when is the deployment window?", above the real
 * answer at 0.49.
 */

